# Requirements-first architecture

A **requirement** is anything that motivates a decision across any repository
(product code, infrastructure, build tooling, tests, and protocols alike): user
intent, architecture and owned code footprint, assumptions, performance or
timing metrics, stated infeasibilities, risks, and implementation work.

Everything an agent works on — a plan, an implemented feature or section of the
codebase, a workflow/dev cycle, or a status report — is expressible as a
unified requirements register (`R1`, `R2`, …) rendered into a standard inline
view, with **two requirement kinds**:

- **`assumption`:** Informs design and is not work (intent, constraints,
  budgets, infeasibility claims, invariants). Carries **no `scope`**.
- **`implementation`:** Something you actually build. Must carry its
  phase-by-phase **`scope`** (`first_pass_min`, `depends_on`, `breakdown` with
  `min` + `why`, `basis`, `risk`). Never create standalone scope or parallelism
  requirements — dependencies live in `scope.depends_on`, and totals plus the
  critical path are derived automatically.

Workflow primitives:

- **Unconstrained initial plans:** Keep initial planning raw and unconstrained
  so the agent can explore unfamiliar areas freely, starting with one top-level
  architecture diagram (`system-diagram`, `architecture` mode) whose component
  IDs define the register sections, plus `flow` diagrams for critical paths.
- **State (`state-requirements`):** Author the JSON register (`R1`, `R2`, …),
  each with a short user/agent `story` headline, one of four statuses
  (`needs-you > user-specified > validated > unconfirmed`, never letting
  `user-specified` hide unverified technical risk), and phase-by-phase `scope`
  on every `implementation` requirement (via `scope-work`), then render and
  validate it with `render_register.py`.
- **Question (`question-requirements`):** Always run in a **fresh subagent**
  with zero prior context. Challenge either the **premise** or the **scope** of
  any requirement (including `user-specified` decisions), run minimal targeted
  spikes in disposable directories or worktrees, and do deep web research for
  open-source replacements. Keeping custom code requires empirical disproof of
  alternatives; reshaping a design to fit an adopted tool beats owning custom
  code.
- **Delete (`delete-requirements`):** Delete unnecessary requirements, custom
  code, and process steps first; adopt proven open-source tools and keep glue
  thin; apply scope edits directly on affected requirements; optimize surviving
  parts; and prove intent and non-regression using the repository's
  verification workflow or tests. Never start or merge custom implementation
  whose requirements have not been questioned and pruned.
- **Interactive default vs autonomous loop:** Standard interactive work lets
  the engineer choose which skill to run next. Explicitly invoke opt-in
  `auto-plan` when you want the autonomous plan → state → question → delete
  loop (stopping after two material-unchanged rounds) followed by
  implementation.

## Code and data as source of truth

Prioritize expressing surviving requirements, assumptions, invariants, and
performance metrics deterministically in code (types, state enums/machines,
runtime assertions, tests, schemas) or structured data (JSON/JSONL receipts,
metric baselines) rather than parallel prose Markdown documents or explanatory
comments that drift out of date.

- A reader inspecting code should be able to understand its surviving
  requirements without reading a separate prose spec.
- Read the code and `git log -p` / `git diff` to understand design decisions
  made over time rather than trusting older Markdown summaries.
- Delete stale prose and comments invalidated by changes instead of maintaining
  them alongside the code.
